'=============================================================================
'
'  Stratus AS3935 Lightning Detection System
'  Logging the lightning records sent by the Raspberry Pi LDS
'
'  Property of METRON (PTY) LTD [Inteltronics]
'  Developer: L.J. Esterhuizen
'
'=============================================================================
' Site: QUAGGASKLIP. Requires CR300 OS 6.0 or later; this unit is on 7.04.
'
' WIRING, on the Terminal 2 Click in socket 2 of the Pi 2 Click Shield
'
'   "INT"   Pi BCM 26   ->  CR300  C2      lightning records in
'   "RST"   Pi BCM 19   ->  CR300  P_SW    strike pulse
'   "GND"   Pi GND      ->  CR300  G       required
'
' Use the INT and RST terminals, NOT "TX". The shield's socket 2 brings INT out on
' BCM 26 and RST on BCM 19, and both are free. "TX" is BCM 14, the Pi's hardware
' UART, which is shared with the USB HUB HAT's CP2102 bridge and is contended. A
' long diagnosis established that records sent on BCM 14 never arrived, through
' three separate transmit methods, while BCM 26 delivers every byte.
'
' The detector does not use the UART peripheral at all. It builds the 9600 baud
' waveform in software with pigpio on whichever GPIO is configured, so any free pin
' works. The unit confirms it at startup:
' "Campbell UART TX enabled on GPIO 26 @ 9600 baud".
'
' Ground is not optional. Without it the receiver samples noise.
'
' Cable screen: land it at the CR300 end only, on signal ground, and cut it back at
' the Pi end. The shield has no earth of its own, so grounding both ends would turn
' the screen into a ground loop conductor.
'
' Pi side, in /home/quaggasklip/lightning_config.json: campbell_uart_enabled true,
' campbell_uart_tx_pin 26, pulse_mirror_enabled true, pulse_mirror_pin 19.
' After editing: sudo systemctl restart lightning-detector
'
' PORT NAME, CR300-SPECIFIC
'
' The port is ComC2_Rx, control terminal 2 configured receive-only. Opening the
' pair as Com1 compiles but received nothing on this unit, while ComC2_Rx delivers.
' ComC1 is the CR6 spelling and does not compile here at all. There is no
' PortPairConfig on the CR300 either: that belongs to the CR1000X and CR6, where
' ports default to 5 V. The CR300 spec lists C1 and C2 as 5.0 V output and 3.3 V
' input, already the level the Pi drives, and P_SW is 3.3 V as well.
'
' Record formats, 9600 baud 8N1, 3.3 V TTL, CR LF terminated:
'
'   L,<distance_km>,<energy>     lightning
'   H,<cpu_temp_c>,<rssi_dbm>    detector health, once a day
'
' distance_km is -1 when the sensor could not range the strike.
'
' Lightning is the only thing stored. H records are recognised and dropped, because
' health belongs to the admin panel, which is the system of record for whether a
' unit is alive. So this logger cannot tell a quiet sky from a dead detector.
'
' WHY SerialIn AND NOT SerialInRecord
'
' SerialInRecord was used first, keyed on the record's first letter as a BeginWord.
' It never framed anything, even once the wiring was proven and all 70 bytes of a
' five record burst were confirmed arriving. Keying on the first byte also made the
' read depend on the one byte a waking port is most likely to drop.
'
' SerialIn needs no BeginWord. It reads until the termination character, so the
' letter stays in the string where CRBasic can test it directly.
'
' TimeOut is 1, being 0.01 s units, NOT 0. Zero means wait indefinitely for the
' terminator or the character limit, which would stall the scan.
'
' TerminationChar 10 is LF as a numeric ASCII code, so it terminates the read and
' is excluded from the result. The CR before it is kept, leaving "L,40,1234567"
' plus a trailing CR, which SplitStr in numeric mode ignores.
'
' The pulse mirror is an independent strike count taken off the sensor interrupt,
' so it does not depend on the serial line. Pulses ahead of serial means the serial
' path is dropping records; serial ahead of pulses means the pulse wire is at fault.
'=============================================================================

Const SCAN_MS = 200
Const LF = 10                ' TerminationChar, numeric so it is excluded
Const MAX_CHARS = 48

Public LastDistanceKm As Float
Public LastEnergy As Long
Public StrikeCount As Long
Public StrikesToday As Long
Public ClosestTodayKm As Float
Public MaxEnergyToday As Long
Public PulseCountScan As Float
Public PulseCountTotal As Float
Public CountMismatch As Float

Public LastRecord As String * 48
Public Parsed(2) As Float
Public ParseErrorCount As Long

' Live only, never sampled into a table, so no health reaches storage. It is a
' positive witness that the wire and ground are good after weeks with no
' lightning, which ParseErrorCount alone cannot tell you.
Public HealthRecordCount As Long

' Link diagnostics, also live only. SerialOpen returns False if the port refuses to
' open, and discarding that makes a refused port indistinguishable from a cut
' cable. SerialInChk counts bytes in the buffer and returns -1 if the port is not
' open.
Public SerialOpenOK As Boolean
Public BytesWaiting As Long

' BytesWaiting alone is a snapshot and it misleads: it is read at the top of the
' scan and the read below empties the buffer immediately after, so it is back to 0
' before anyone looks at the table, whether bytes arrived or not. These accumulate
' instead. MaxBytesWaiting above 0 proves bytes reached C2 even if nothing parsed,
' and it is what finally showed the BCM 26 wiring delivering all 70 bytes of a five
' record burst.
Public MaxBytesWaiting As Long
Public BytesSeenTotal As Long

Dim EventRowWritten As Boolean

'=============================================================================
' DATA TABLES
'
' Distance, energy and the independent pulse count. Nothing else is stored.
' PulseCountTotal is IEEE4 because it is cumulative and FP2 stops at 7999, where it
' would saturate and also become indistinguishable from NAN.
'=============================================================================

DataTable (LightningEvents, True, -1)
  Sample (1, LastDistanceKm, FP2)
  Sample (1, LastEnergy, Long)
  Sample (1, PulseCountTotal, IEEE4)
EndTable

DataTable (LightningDaily, True, -1)
  DataInterval (0, 1, Day, 10)
  Sample (1, StrikesToday, Long)
  Sample (1, ClosestTodayKm, FP2)
  Sample (1, MaxEnergyToday, Long)
  Totalize (1, PulseCountScan, IEEE4, False)
EndTable

'=============================================================================
' PROGRAM
'=============================================================================

BeginProg
  ClosestTodayKm = NAN
  MaxEnergyToday = 0
  StrikesToday = 0

  SerialOpenOK = SerialOpen (ComC2_Rx, 9600, 0, 0, 256)
  SerialFlush (ComC2_Rx)

  Scan (SCAN_MS, mSec, 0, 0)

    EventRowWritten = False

    ' PConfig 2 = switch closure, POption 0 = counts.
    PulseCount (PulseCountScan, 1, P_SW, 2, 0, 1, 0)
    PulseCountTotal = PulseCountTotal + PulseCountScan

    ' Buffer depth before the read consumes it, plus a running record so a burst
    ' is not invisible by the time the table is read.
    BytesWaiting = SerialInChk (ComC2_Rx)
    If BytesWaiting > MaxBytesWaiting Then MaxBytesWaiting = BytesWaiting
    If BytesWaiting > 0 Then BytesSeenTotal = BytesSeenTotal + BytesWaiting

    ' Cleared first: SerialIn leaves the destination alone when nothing arrives,
    ' so without this the previous record would be reprocessed every scan.
    LastRecord = ""
    SerialIn (LastRecord, ComC2_Rx, 1, LF, MAX_CHARS)

    ' Two characters, not one: "L," tests the record's shape rather than just its
    ' first byte. Testing only for "L" would also accept a corrupted line such as
    ' "LL,1,2" and log its numbers as a real strike.
    If Len (LastRecord) > 1 Then

      If Left (LastRecord, 2) = "L," Then

        ' SplitOption 0 is NUMERIC: it keeps + - . 0-9 E and treats everything
        ' else as a delimiter, so the leading letter and the trailing CR cost
        ' nothing and the two values land in Parsed(1) and Parsed(2).
        SplitStr (Parsed(1), LastRecord, ",", 2, 0)

        ' x = x is false only for NAN, which catches a failed parse.
        If Parsed(1) = Parsed(1) AND Parsed(2) = Parsed(2) Then
          LastDistanceKm = Parsed(1)
          LastEnergy = Parsed(2)
          StrikeCount = StrikeCount + 1
          StrikesToday = StrikesToday + 1

          If LastEnergy > MaxEnergyToday Then MaxEnergyToday = LastEnergy

          ' -1 means unrangeable, so it is not a closest-of-day candidate.
          If LastDistanceKm >= 0 Then
            If ClosestTodayKm <> ClosestTodayKm Then
              ClosestTodayKm = LastDistanceKm
            ElseIf LastDistanceKm < ClosestTodayKm Then
              ClosestTodayKm = LastDistanceKm
            EndIf
          EndIf

          EventRowWritten = True
          CallTable LightningEvents
        Else
          ParseErrorCount = ParseErrorCount + 1
        EndIf

      ElseIf Left (LastRecord, 2) = "H," Then

        ' Counted live, then dropped. Deliberately NOT added to ParseErrorCount:
        ' routine traffic accumulating in the fault counter would hide a missing
        ' ground or a baud mismatch.
        HealthRecordCount = HealthRecordCount + 1

      Else
        ParseErrorCount = ParseErrorCount + 1
      EndIf
    EndIf

    CountMismatch = PulseCountTotal - StrikeCount

    ' A pulse with no serial record still deserves a row: the pulse is the hardware
    ' witness that the sensor fired. Gated on EventRowWritten so it cannot double
    ' up with the strike row above.
    If PulseCountScan > 0 And Not EventRowWritten Then
      CallTable LightningEvents
    EndIf

    ' Called every scan on purpose: Totalize accumulates once per CallTable, so
    ' gating this on IfTime would build the daily total from one sample a day.
    ' DataInterval decides when the row is written.
    CallTable LightningDaily

    ' Reset today's figures after the daily row is written above.
    If IfTime (0, 1, Day) Then
      StrikesToday = 0
      ClosestTodayKm = NAN
      MaxEnergyToday = 0
    EndIf

  NextScan
EndProg
